\DOC ENSURES_WHILE_UP_TAC

\TYPE {ENSURES_WHILE_UP_TAC : term -> term -> term -> term -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Reduces an {ensures} goal describing a counted loop to a single conjunctive
goal containing a nonzero trip-count condition plus {ensures} subspecifications
for loop entry, body, backedge and exit.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes four term arguments: {count}, the loop trip count (a
numeral-valued term); {pc1}, the program counter at the top of the loop body;
{pc2}, the program counter at the loop backedge/test; and {inv}, a loop
invariant given as a lambda abstraction {\i s. ...} over the iteration index
and the machine state. Applied to an {ensures} goal, it produces one subgoal
whose conclusion is a conjunction of five parts: {~(count = 0)}; an {ensures}
from the original precondition to {inv} instantiated at {0} at {pc1}; a
{forall i. ...} body step from {inv} at {i} at {pc1} to {inv} at {i + 1} at
{pc2}, guarded by hypotheses bounding {i} below {count} and asserting {count}
is positive; a similarly quantified backedge step from {inv} at {i} at {pc2}
back to {inv} at {i} at {pc1}, guarded by the same bounds together with
{0 < i}; and an {ensures} from {inv} instantiated at {count} at {pc2} to the
original postcondition. The frame ({MAYCHANGE ...}) of the original goal is
reused in every component.
{
   A ?- t
   =================================  ENSURES_WHILE_UP_TAC count pc1 pc2 (\i s. ...)
   A ?- u
}
\noindent where {u} is the four-part conjunction described above together with
the added nonzero-count conjunct.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-subgoal transformation of {ensures}
goals for the x86 model.

\ENDDOC
